                 current form and the alternatives; how a jurisdiction over
                 150,000 may change its own structure; impacts on the School
                 Board and constitutional officers; impacts of converting to
                 city status. Attachment A compares the six county forms.
                 Carries no history: every episode appears as precedent, never
                 as narrative. Four points bear on this report. Note 8 (p.7):
                 the County Manager Plan "appears to be the only county form of
                 government that does not refer to its Board members as
                 'supervisors'", and Arlington adopted the Plan by referendum
                 in 1930, operating under it since 1932 - so the name, the
                 at-large method and the five seats arrive together. Va. Code s
                 15.2-705 requires at-large election of all members, the only
                 form that does. Note 13 (p.20) gives the reported citations
                 for both Vollin actions. Note 7 (p.6) cites Bennett v. Garrett
